> Doing this with atomic actions is IMO not feasible, which is why there is a lock. This allows the parallel states to be managed mostly independently.
Prefer  to avoid a global lock on all work items. Can we use above proposed LOCKED_bit to implement an individual k_work item lock (in pseudo-code)?
```
on_delayq_timer_expiry() {
/* Pre-condition: DELAYED_bit is set. */
   work=peak(delay_q);
   atomic_t mask=(1<<LOCKED_bit);
   while (atomic_or(&work.flags, mask) {/*do nothing*/}  //A work-item specific barrier
   pop_delay_q(work_item);
   call insert_on_run_q(work_item);  
}
call_insert_on_run_q(k_work w) {
  /* check cancel bit is unset , and set QUEUED_bit */
  if (! atomic_test_and_clear_bit(w.flags, DELAYED_bit) { 
      /* work-item specific barrier identical to above */
  }
  /* insert into run_q ...*/
  atomic_clear_bit(w.flags, LOCKED_bit);  //End of item-specific barrier
}
```
Functions for _cancel(), _schedule() and _reschedule() would have similar barrier. Just as in your current implementation, idea is to defer processing modification to an "in-process" work-item until it is on a queue, or it is running.

